{
  "teamId": 142,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Minnesota Twins spent the better part of six months convincing themselves — and anyone watching — that they were a playoff team. From the opening weeks, the offense flashed genuine pop: Ryan Jeffers launched a three-run shot on April 3rd to set the tone, Byron Buxton reminded everyone what a healthy Buck looks like with a walk-off blast on April 23rd, and Alex Kirilloff's three-run homer on May 25th was the kind of swing that makes a fan base believe. Through June, the Twins rode a .560 winning percentage, sitting comfortably above .500 and squarely in the wild card conversation.\n\nBut the season had a shadow side that never fully went away. The bullpen — Griffin Jax and Steven Okert in particular — kept surfacing in the worst moments. Okert surrendered a Cal Raleigh grand slam in May, then gave up a Jurickson Profar three-run shot in August. Jax seemed to find new ways to break hearts, from a Cedric Mullins two-run homer in April to a Kyle Manzardo two-run blast in mid-September. Jhoan Duran, the ace of the 'pen, had his own rough nights, including a brutal Will Brennan three-run homer in a tight Cleveland game. These weren't isolated stumbles — they were a recurring fault line running through an otherwise competitive roster.\n\nThe season's most emotional moment came on September 1st, when a returning Royce Lewis — battling back from injury yet again — launched a go-ahead two-run homer that registered as the highest-leverage positive play of the entire year. It briefly felt like fate was writing a comeback story. But September had other plans. The Twins went 9-18 down the stretch, their worst month by a wide margin, as the rotation frayed, the bullpen continued to bleed, and late-game collapses piled up. A Lars Nootbaar walk-off single off Duran on August 25th, a throwing error that let Kansas City back into a September game, a three-run homer surrendered to a Red Sox bench player — the losses came in gut-punch fashion.\n\nWhen Brooks Lee's double scored two runs on September 26th in one of the final games of the year, it was a small, bittersweet grace note on a season that ended two wins short of meaningful. The Twins finished 82-80, their runs scored and allowed nearly identical, a team that was exactly as good as their record suggested — good enough to tease, not good enough to advance. A winter of roster decisions awaited a franchise that clearly had pieces, but just as clearly had holes that October exposed before they even got there.",
